cuadmm_exe.patch: the changes to cuADMM that CUADMM_SEDUMI needs.

cuADMM (https://github.com/ComputationalRobotics/cuADMM) is a first-order SDP
solver for CUDA GPUs. This patch is made against its commit fcadc30. Upstream,
cuadmm_exe fixes the tolerance (1e-4) and the iteration limit (1e6) and writes
no solution; CUADMM_SEDUMI passes both and reads the solution back.

Changes:
  src/main.cu        the arguments <prefix> [tol] [max_iter] [eig_streams]
                     [dump_sol] [switch_admm] [sig] [sigscale] [sig_thresh];
                     with dump_sol = 1, the files X_opt.txt, y_opt.txt and
                     S_opt.txt; the line CUADMM_TIMING, which marks a
                     completed solve (the exit status is 0 even when CUDA
                     fails), and the line CUADMM_MEM of GPU memory use.
  include/cuadmm/memory.h
                     the solution files written from the heap (a stack array
                     segfaults past about 1e6 entries) and in %.17g.
  include/cuadmm/cholesky_cpu.h
                     the diagonal shift of A*A' relative to its largest
                     diagonal entry: with the fixed shift 1e-16 the
                     factorization fails when the equality constraints are
                     linearly dependent.
  include/lapack.h   (new) the LP64 LAPACK routine dsyevd, for systems
                     without MATLAB's lapack.h.
  CMakeLists.txt     LAPACK in place of MATLAB; Ubuntu's SuiteSparse paths.

On Ubuntu or Debian, including Ubuntu under WSL, cuadmm_install.sh, in this
folder, installs the missing packages and carries out the steps below.
Elsewhere, build on Linux or under WSL, with the CUDA toolkit, CMake, BLAS,
LAPACK and SuiteSparse (CHOLMOD, CAMD, CCOLAMD) installed:

  git clone https://github.com/ComputationalRobotics/cuADMM.git
  cd cuADMM
  git checkout fcadc30
  git submodule update --init
  git apply <this folder>/cuadmm_exe.patch
  cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
        -DCMAKE_CUDA_FLAGS="-gencode arch=compute_89,code=sm_89"
  cmake --build build --target cuadmm_exe -j8

The CUDA flags name the compute capability of the GPU (89: RTX 40 series,
80: A100, 90: H100); without them nvcc compiles for its default
architecture, and the driver JIT-compiles that code at load. Configuring
downloads googletest. CUADMM_SEDUMI looks for ~/cuADMM/build/cuadmm_exe;
for a build elsewhere, set the environment variable CUADMM_EXE to the path
of build/cuadmm_exe as the launcher sees it (under WSL, /home/... or
~/...), or pass it to CUADMM_SEDUMI as pars.exe.

