+// usage: cuadmm_exe <prefix> [stop_tol] [max_iter] [eig_streams] [dump_sol]
+//                            [switch_admm] [sig] [sigscale] [sig_thresh]
+//
+// Everything past <prefix> was hardcoded upstream (tol 1e-4, max_iter 1e6,
+// sig 1e2, standard ADMM).  A caller needs the tolerance and the iteration
+// limit at least: the time per iteration does not depend on the tolerance, the
+// iteration count does (334, 4417, 12917, 126344 iterations at 1e-4, 1e-5,
+// 1e-6, 1e-7 on one measured SDP).
+//   switch_admm - iteration at which to switch to sGS-ADMM (0 = never, the
+//                 upstream default; upstream's own commented line used 5000)
+//   sig/sigscale/sig_thresh - the penalty parameter and its update schedule
+// dump_sol = 1 writes X, y and S to <prefix>X_opt.txt, y_opt.txt, S_opt.txt, so
+// the caller can check the residual and the PSD blocks of the point itself.
+// The CUADMM_TIMING line marks a completed solve: the exit status is 0 even
+// when every CUDA call fails.
+//
+// MEMORY INSTRUMENTATION (CUADMM_MEM lines).  These problems are expected to be
+// memory-limited before they are time-limited, so peak memory is a first-class
+// measurement.  WSL does NOT report per-process GPU memory -- nvidia-smi
+// --query-compute-apps returns no rows -- and the Windows desktop already holds
+// 2.5-3.8 GiB that drifts, so sampling nvidia-smi cannot attribute usage to this
+// process.  cudaMemGetInfo deltas can: a baseline is taken AFTER the CUDA
+// context exists but BEFORE any solver allocation, and every later reading is
+// reported as a delta from it.  Peak memory is an INIT-TIME property, so this
+// can be measured with a handful of iterations instead of a full solve.
+static size_t gpu_used_bytes()
+{
+    size_t freeB = 0, totalB = 0;
+    if (cudaMemGetInfo(&freeB, &totalB) != cudaSuccess) return 0;
+    return totalB - freeB;
+}
